-- ('The most depressing aspect of the lives of the couples you watch surreptitiously arguing in restaurants is that they are almost always unaware of the true subject of argument.', '你在餐厅里偷偷观察到的那些争吵夫妇生活中最令人沮丧的一面是，他们几乎总是不知道争论的真正主题。', 27),
('It seems that it is the most unsuccessful people who give the most advice, particularly for writing and financial matters.', '似乎最不成功的人给出最多建议，特别是在写作和财务问题上。', 27),
('Rumors are only valuable when they are denied.', '谣言只有在被否认时才有价值。', 27),
-- ('Over the long term, you are more likely to fool yourself than others.', '从长远来看，你更可能愚弄自己而不是别人。', 27),
('There are two types of people: those who try to win and those who try to win arguments. They are never the same.', '有两种人：试图获胜的人和试图赢得争论的人。他们永远不是同一种人。', 27),
('People usually apologize so they can do it again.', '人们通常道歉是为了能够再次犯错。', 27),
('Mathematics is to knowledge what an artificial hand is to the real one; some amputate to replace.', '数学之于知识，就像假手之于真手；有些人截肢是为了替换。', 27),
('Modernity inflicts a sucker narrative on activities; now we "walk for exercise," not "walk" with no justification; for hidden reasons.', '现代性给活动强加了一种愚蠢的叙述；现在我们"为了锻炼而走路"，而不是无需理由地"走路"；出于隐藏的原因。', 27),
('Social media are severely antisocial, health foods are empirically unhealthy, knowledge workers are very ignorant, and social sciences aren''t scientific at all.', '社交媒体严重反社交，健康食品在经验上不健康，知识工作者非常无知，社会科学根本不科学。', 27),
('For so many, instead of looking for "cause of death" when they expire, we should be looking for "cause of life" when they are still around.', '对于很多人来说，与其在他们死后寻找"死因"，不如在他们还活着时寻找"生因"。', 27),
('It is those who use others who are the most upset when someone uses them.', '利用他人的人在被人利用时最为愤怒。', 27),
-- ('If someone gives you more than one reason why he wants the job, don''t hire him.', '如果有人给你超过一个他想要这份工作的理由，不要雇用他。', 27),
('Failure of secondorder thinking: he tells you a secret and somehow expects you to keep it, when he just gave you evidence that he can''t keep it himself.', '二阶思维的失败：他告诉你一个秘密，却期望你保守秘密，而他刚刚给了你证据证明他自己都守不住秘密。', 27),
('Social networks present information about what people like; more informative if, instead, they described what they don''t like.', '社交网络展示人们喜欢什么的信息；如果相反，它们描述人们不喜欢什么，会更有信息量。', 27),
('People are so prone to overcausation that you can make the reticent turn loquacious by dropping an occasional "why?" in the conversation.', '人们如此倾向于过度因果化，以至于你可以通过在对话中偶尔抛出一个"为什么？"来让沉默寡言的人变得健谈。', 27),
('I need to keep reminding myself that a truly independent thinker may look like an accountant.', '我需要不断提醒自己，真正独立的思考者可能看起来像个会计师。', 27);